The Security Council,

Recalling its previous resolutions on international terrorism and the threat it poses to the situation in Afghanistan, in particular its resolutions 1267 (1999), 1988 (2011), 2082 (2012), 2160 (2014), 2255 (2015), 2501 (2019), 2557 (2020), 2611 (2021), 2615 (2021), 2665 (2022), 2716 (2023), 2734 (2024), and 2763 (2024), as well as other relevant resolutions,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity, and national unity of Afghanistan,

Reaffirming its unequivocal condemnation of all terrorist acts in all their forms and manifestations, committed by whomever, wherever, and for whatever purposes,

Expressing its serious concern over the ongoing presence of and threats posed by terrorist groups in Afghanistan, in particular the Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant - Khorasan Province (ISIL-K), Al-Qaida, and other affiliated groups, and deeply concerned by their continued efforts and demonstrated capacity to plan and conduct attacks within Afghanistan, in the wider region, and globally,

Reiterating that the territory of Afghanistan must never be used to threaten or attack any country, or to shelter, recruit, or train terrorists, or to plan or finance terrorist acts, in accordance with resolution 2593 (2021),

Emphasizing the vital importance of the sanctions regime established pursuant to resolution 1988 (2011) in promoting peace, stability, and security in Afghanistan and countering terrorism,

Recalling the expectations that the Taliban adhere to their commitments, including on counter-terrorism, upholding human rights and fundamental freedoms, particularly the rights of women and girls to education, work, and public life, and the establishment of an inclusive and representative governance system,

Expressing concern over the illicit production and trafficking of narcotics and synthetic drugs, illegal exploitation of natural resources, and illicit financial flows, and their nexus with the financing of terrorism,

Taking note of the reports of the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Team, and recognizing the essential role played by the Monitoring Team in supporting the 1988 Sanctions Committee,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides, in order to conduct its work, that the 1988 Committee shall continue to be supported by the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Team established pursuant to paragraph 7 of resolution 1526 (2004) for a period of twelve months from the date of expiration of the current mandate in December 2025;

2. Directs the Monitoring Team to gather information on instances of non-compliance with the measures imposed in resolution 2255 (2015) and to keep the Committee informed of such instances, as well as to facilitate, upon request by Member States, capacity-building on the implementation of the measures;

3. Directs the Monitoring Team to continue to monitor illicit drug production and trafficking, illicit exploitation of natural resources, and illicit financial flows, and the nexus between such criminal activities and the financing of terrorism;

4. Requests the Monitoring Team to actively track and report on the evolving regional and transnational threat posed by terrorist groups operating in or from Afghanistan, notably ISIL-K and Al-Qaida, including foreign terrorist fighters and their networks, recruitment, and financing mechanisms;

5. Requests the Monitoring Team to continue to cooperate closely with the United Nations Assistance Mission in Afghanistan (UNAMA) and relevant United Nations counter-terrorism bodies and sanctions committees;

6. Encourages Member States, particularly regional States, to enhance cooperation and share relevant information with the Committee and the Monitoring Team to support the effective implementation of the sanctions measures;

7. Requests the Monitoring Team to provide the Committee with regular briefings and written reports on its findings, including recommendations on potential updates to the sanctions list;

8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

The Security Council,

Recalling its previous resolutions on international terrorism and the threat it poses to the situation in Afghanistan, in particular its resolutions 1267 (1999), 1988 (2011), 2082 (2012), 2160 (2014), 2255 (2015), 2501 (2019), 2557 (2020), 2611 (2021), 2615 (2021), 2664 (2022), 2665 (2022), 2716 (2023), 2734 (2024), and 2763 (2024), as well as other relevant resolutions,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity, and national unity of Afghanistan,

Reaffirming its unequivocal condemnation of all terrorist acts in all their forms and manifestations, committed by whomever, wherever, and for whatever purposes,

Reaffirming that Member States must ensure that any measures taken to combat terrorism comply with all their obligations under international law, in particular international human rights law, international refugee law, and international humanitarian law,

Expressing its serious concern over the ongoing presence of and threats posed by terrorist groups in Afghanistan, in particular the Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant - Khorasan Province (ISIL-K), Al-Qaida, and other affiliated groups, and deeply concerned by their continued efforts and demonstrated capacity to plan and conduct attacks within Afghanistan, in the wider region, and globally,

Reiterating that the territory of Afghanistan must never be used to threaten or attack any country, or to shelter, recruit, or train terrorists, or to plan or finance terrorist acts, in accordance with resolution 2593 (2021),

Emphasizing the vital importance of the sanctions regime established pursuant to resolution 1988 (2011) in promoting peace, stability, and security in Afghanistan and countering terrorism,

Recalling the expectations that the Taliban adhere to their commitments, including on counter-terrorism, upholding human rights and fundamental freedoms, particularly the rights of women and girls to education, work, and public life, and the establishment of an inclusive and representative governance system,

Reaffirming the importance of addressing Afghanistan's dire humanitarian and economic challenges, and recalling resolution 2615 (2021) in this regard,

Expressing concern over the illicit production and trafficking of narcotics and synthetic drugs, illegal exploitation of natural resources, and illicit financial flows, and their nexus with the financing of terrorism,

Taking note of the reports of the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Team, recognizing the essential role played by the Monitoring Team in supporting the 1988 Sanctions Committee, and stressing the importance of the Monitoring Team conducting its work in an objective, impartial, and non-politicized manner,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides, in order to conduct its work, that the 1988 Committee shall continue to be supported by the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Team established pursuant to paragraph 7 of resolution 1526 (2004) for a period of twelve months from the date of expiration of the current mandate in December 2025;

2. Directs the Monitoring Team to gather information on instances of non-compliance with the measures imposed in resolution 2255 (2015), to conduct visits to Afghanistan as appropriate to consult with relevant interlocutors, and to keep the Committee informed of such instances, as well as to facilitate, upon request by Member States, capacity-building on the implementation of the measures;

3. Directs the Monitoring Team to continue to monitor illicit drug production and trafficking, illicit exploitation of natural resources, and illicit financial flows, and the nexus between such criminal activities and the financing of terrorism, in line with international standards on combating the financing of terrorism and anti-money laundering;

4. Requests the Monitoring Team to actively track and report on the evolving regional and transnational threat posed by terrorist groups operating in or from Afghanistan, notably ISIL-K and Al-Qaida, as well as other terrorist groups and networks, including foreign terrorist fighters, their recruitment, including the gender dimensions of terrorist recruitment and narratives, and financing mechanisms;

5. Requests the Monitoring Team to continue to cooperate closely with the United Nations Assistance Mission in Afghanistan (UNAMA) and relevant United Nations counter-terrorism bodies and sanctions committees, and encourages the Monitoring Team to maintain practical engagement with all relevant stakeholders, including through visits to Afghanistan;

6. Encourages Member States, particularly regional States, to enhance cooperation and share relevant information with the Committee and the Monitoring Team to support the effective implementation of the sanctions measures;

7. Requests the Monitoring Team to provide the Committee with regular briefings and written reports on its findings, including objective, evidence-based recommendations on potential updates to the sanctions list, while preserving the Member State-driven nature of listing and delisting processes, and expresses its readiness to review the implementation of the measures taken under resolution 1988 (2011) and make adjustments, as necessary, to support peace, stability, and counter-terrorism efforts in Afghanistan;

8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

